  "briefing": {
    "headline": "The simulation predicts that two work-from-home days a week would cut leavers over the next 12 months by about a third, from 603 to 408 out of 5,000 (roughly 195 fewer departures). Treat the direction as more reliable than the exact figure.",
    "most_at_risk": "Long commuters (-37%) and caregivers (-38%) gain the most because they get several hours a week back for family, while short commuters and long-tenured managers like the change but say it will not affect whether they stay.",
    "risks": [
      "Managers may informally discourage using the home days, and if people feel watched or penalised the retention gain disappears. One manager already said he would 'rather have it in writing than hear about it in a meeting.'",
      "Expectations will rise. Several staff call this a start rather than enough ('I wish they'd gone further', 'a bit more flexibility'), so scaling back later would likely hurt more than never offering it.",
      "Long-commute parents will still take a better outside offer. The policy lowers the urge to job-hunt but does not lock people in, so pay and career competitiveness still matter."
    ],
    "design_changes": [
      "Publish the policy in writing as a formal entitlement with a stated minimum period, for example 12 months, so staff trust it and it is not left to each manager's discretion.",
      "Tell managers plainly that working from home must not affect reviews or promotion. Then check review and promotion outcomes for people who use the home days against those who do not.",
      "Roll it out in stages or keep a comparison group where practical, and track actual quits by commute length and caregiving status. That turns this estimate into real evidence before the policy is locked in company-wide."
    ],
    "confidence": "medium, because the direction is consistent across every group and fits what staff say, but the size is uncertain. It rests on 48 modelled voices, and short commuters and managers say the change won't affect whether they stay, yet the model still gives them a reduction of about a third."
  },
